| `make fe-audit` `[read]` | Judges each frontend app's `npm audit --json` against the accepted risks `config/project.json` records, through `scripts/audit_dependencies.py`: it fails a critical or high finding no accepted row covers, a row past its review day, a row dated more than `dependency_audit.max_review_days` days out and a row whose advisory the audit no longer reports, and lists a weaker uncovered finding without failing. A finding left open for a reason is recorded once, with the reason and the day it must be looked at again, instead of in a note nothing reads. | `make fe-audit` | [`FE_APPS`](settings.md#make-variables), [`dependency_audit.accepted.<app>`](settings.md#configprojectjson), [`dependency_audit.fail_at`](settings.md#configprojectjson), [`dependency_audit.max_review_days`](settings.md#configprojectjson), [`dependency_audit.max_shown_chars`](settings.md#configprojectjson), [`dependency_audit.timeout_seconds`](settings.md#configprojectjson) | Sends each app's lockfile to the npm registry and writes nothing in the tree; npm writes its own per-user log and cache. It is not a `make verify` goal: the registry's answer changes from day to day, and a gate must not. npm that cannot answer, or answers a report it cannot trust (counts that disagree with its entries, or no audited dependency while `package.json` declares one, as an empty lockfile gives), is exit 11 with npm's own message; an app with no `package-lock.json` is exit 2 naming `make fe-install`. Not for installing or fixing packages: `make fe-install`. | [deterministic-checks.md](../guides/deterministic-checks.md#16-dependency-audit-and-accepted-risks--scriptsaudit_dependenciespy) | always |
